diff --git a/envs/drone_vla_orbit.py b/envs/drone_vla_orbit.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6f36bee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/envs/drone_vla_orbit.py
@@ -0,0 +1,745 @@
+# Added in this project (2026-09-21): orbit-the-box inspection task, scene sunny_baseball_field.
+#
+# Task: keep the nose (camera) pointed at the box and fly a horizontal circle of radius R at height H with speed V.
+#   The language instruction sets the direction: [Task 0] clockwise, [Task 1] counter-clockwise (seen from above).
+#   "Stop after one lap" is not part of the policy: the policy only learns to keep orbiting. On the drone the onboard
+#   program accumulates the nose yaw (the nose always points at the box, so 360 deg = one lap), decides when the lap
+#   is done and switches to position hold.
+#   Reason: the policy sees neither x/y position nor time, and the start and the end of a lap look exactly the same,
+#   so it could not both depart and stop.
+#
+# Inherits DroneVLALongTaskEnv and overrides only __init__, init_sim, set_task_eval, reset,
+# calculateObservations and calculateReward; step / image features / VLM / saving come from the parent class.
+#
+# Differences from the parent class (upstream GRaD-Nav++ long task) and why:
+#   1. The camera is rendered with the attitude (x,-y,-z,w). Upstream negates y and z of the position before the
+#      renderer but not the quaternion, so the camera looks in the mirror image of the body heading (body yaw psi,
+#      camera looks at -psi). Verified numerically on 2026-09-21: the dynamics and the obstacle cone use the correct
+#      (cos psi, sin psi); only rendering and quaternion_yaw_forward use (cos psi, -sin psi).
+#      The original tasks keep yaw near 0, so it never showed; an orbit turns 360 deg, and without the fix the nose
+#      points at the box while the camera does not see it.
+#   2. The reward's nose direction uses the correct rotation formula (not utils.rotation.quaternion_yaw_forward,
+#      same reason).
+#   3. The thrust penalty is relative to this airframe's hover command (upstream hard-codes 0.42 = the hover value of
+#      the original drone carl; this airframe is about 0.24), and each environment only counts its own (upstream adds
+#      a torch.sum over the whole batch to every environment).
+#   4. prev_lin_vel is updated every step (upstream never updates it, it stays 0, so the "acceleration penalty" is
+#      really a velocity penalty that fights the orbit speed).
+#   5. No A* reference trajectory or waypoints (upstream picks "the next point 0.5 m further in x", assuming travel
+#      along +x, which fails on a circle); instead an orbit velocity field: tangential speed V plus corrections that
+#      pull back to radius R and height H.
+#   6. The attitude penalty covers tilt only (roll/pitch), not yaw (upstream penalises the difference to the start
+#      quaternion, and an orbit necessarily turns 360 deg).
+#   7. Each episode starts at a random angle on the circle, nose at the box.
+#   8. Episodes end early closer than 1.0 m or farther than 5.0 m from the box centre (upstream has no collision
+#      termination; this only stops clearly lost episodes from wasting samples, obstacle avoidance itself is still
+#      GRaD-Nav++'s distance reward).
+#   9. The "body rate too high" termination reads privilege_obs_buf[:, 13:16] (body rates). Upstream reads 10:13, the
+#      quaternion: its norm is always 1, below the threshold 15, so the condition never fired.
+#
+# v2 (2026-09-26): the policy from the first training run (v1) never completed a lap (0.24 m/s, only about 170 deg in
+# 35 s, 15 cm low). Three fixes, each behind an env_hyper switch; absent = v1 behaviour (old checkpoints, evaluations
+# and deployment reference files stay reproducible). The v2 training config enables all three:
+#   ORBIT_PROGRESS_REWARD (v1 = 0): orbit progress reward = weight x clip(tangential speed in the commanded
+#      direction / V, -1, 1). In v1 only track (squared velocity error) encouraged orbiting: hovering still on the
+#      circle lost only 0.5 per step, and the push weakened near the target speed. Summed over an episode the
+#      progress term equals the arc flown, i.e. the "one lap" reward paid out per step (a one-off "lap done" reward has
+#      zero gradient in differentiable RL, and the critic cannot see how far it has gone: start and one-lap positions
+#      are the same).
+#   ORBIT_OBST_MIN_Z (v1 = no filter): drop obstacle points below this z (ground, grass, 3DGS noise under the ground).
+#      v1's obstacle term pays +d when d < 0.95 m and counted the ground too: flying 15 cm low earned about +0.84 per
+#      step for free, more than orbiting did.
+#   ORBIT_OBS_FRAME (v1 = 'world'): with 'heading' the policy's attitude is the quaternion with yaw removed (w >= 0)
+#      and its velocity is in the heading frame (forward, left, up). v1 used the world quaternion: one lap flips its
+#      sign (q -> -q), all training starts have w <= 0, and after passing 0 deg (east of the box) the policy sees a
+#      form it never trained on and gets stuck there.
+#      The new form is independent of world yaw: an ideal orbit looks the same anywhere on the circle (only the image
+#      differs), and on the drone there is no need to align with PX4's yaw.
+#      The critic's attitude fields become the continuous, unique [nose direction hx, hy, body up-vector x, y] and its
+#      velocity moves to the heading frame (the VAE's velocity target reads the same field, so it stays consistent).
+#   Also (both versions, no effect on reward values): calculateReward reads position / velocity / attitude from the
+#   state tensors stored by calculateObservations (the v2 critic fields are no longer world-frame); per-term rewards
+#   are stored in self.last_reward_terms and accumulated for the training log (pop_reward_term_means).
+#
+# Real-drone latency (2026-10-19): v2's first real flight oscillated up and down. With LATENCY_MODEL (dict) enabled in
+#   env_hyper, the physics goes through envs/assets/command_latency.py ("command delay + motor response of the
+#   thrust"); absent or enabled not True = the original simulator (step() uses the original run_simulation, step for
+#   step identical). Observations, rewards and starts are unchanged. Details in the header of command_latency.py.
+#
+# v4 (2026-10-29): two switches; absent = v3 behaviour (step for step identical).
+#   HEIGHT_INTEGRATOR (dict, created only with enabled: True): adds a height integral f_I to the thrust
+#      (envs/assets/height_integrator.py), removing v3's constant height offset (about 1.1 cm lower for every 1% of
+#      extra mass). The onboard program must do the same.
+#   ORBIT_START_MODE ('upstream' = original behaviour): 'deploy' / 'deploy_warm' start each episode like reset() in
+#      orbit_policy/orbit_runtime.py on the drone: the start thrust command is the nominal hover f_hover_prog (upstream
+#      gives every reset drone the hover fraction of drone 0); the VAE history starts over ('deploy': all zero;
+#      'deploy_warm': 5 copies of the post-reset observation); the action low-pass filter of the training algorithm
+#      restarts too ('deploy': the first step uses the raw action; 'deploy_warm': starts from the hover action).
+#      Upstream never clears the low-pass filter or the VAE history between episodes, so a new episode starts with the
+#      previous episode's data, unlike the drone.
